## Summary
In practice, property-based testing (PBT) is not only used for testing public API’s, but it is also used to verify the correctness of an internal implementation. It can find bugs because it takes a broad range of possible inputs while generating test cases. By using data-driven testing (DDT), you can find different paths through your code.

Unit Test, Integration Test, and Property-based Testing are different forms of testing but they often coexist in the lifecycle of building quality software. Combining them gives you an edge in ensuring that your software behaves as expected under a wide range of conditions.

In order to increase coverage, testing should always be performed with the intention of finding issues and not just to pass tests. Good testing should be able to highlight areas of risk and help developers understand the behaviour of the different components of the software system.

By integrating the above principles into the development process (i.e., write tests early, regularly run tests, and strive for code coverage), we can have confidence in the reliability of the software. Additionally, the principle of testing on the smallest possible program ensures we address edge cases early on in the development process which in turn leads to more robust applications.

Happy Coding !


















































