As you can see, the PUT_ContainsContract method is marked as [PexAssumeUnderTest]. 
The method takes three parameters: the list to add elements to, and the element to be checked. However, the assumption under test is made with [PexAssumeUnderTest]. This indicates that this test method is not supposed to change the state of the world. It should only test the expected behavior of the method. The PexInstrumenter will not generate tests that involve this method changing the state of the application under test. 

In simple words, [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute is used to denote that a method does not change its state when it is executed. It is commonly used in unit testing in conjunction with Pex. This allows Pex to focus on testing the state transition logic and behavior when these methods are called.

Here's how you would use it:

```csharp
public class MyTestClass
{
        [PexMethod]
        [PexAssumeUnderTest]
        public void MyTestMethod(int x)
        {
            // some code that does something with x
        }
}
```

When dealing with methods like these, Pex and the other testing tools will help you generate test cases where the method is called, and where its state is irrelevant to the test. They will not focus on state changes caused by the method call.

Reference:
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/pex/contracts.aspx
The official Microsoft research site for Pex and testing tools like these offer detailed information and examples. Click on "Pex Contracts" and it will give a very good overview of the different types of contracts that you can use with Pex and the attributes that can be applied to them. They can both complement to produce highly effective tests.

NOTE: [PexAssumeUnderTest] can used with methods that does not affect the system under test state. Pex does not generate tests with methods marked as [PexAssumeUnderTest] changing the state of the application under test.
