``` 

In the test `PUT_ContainsContract`, we make our assumption on the condition we are testing under the array list. We also store the old index of the x in the array list before any operation was made. After performing the operation `contains`, we check if the index of element x in array list is not equal to the old index, if it is not the case then contains is working correctly.

In the test we are also checking if the contrat `ContractAssert.SucceedsRetrievalContract<object>(arrList, Old_x);` holds true for every element of the array in the set.

Finally we perform an assertion with a property inference that states "The method .Contains(T ) must behave in the same way on the original collection" which is true as per the ArrayList .Contains method's behavior.

Testing under the arrayList shows that ArrayList’s `Contains` behaviour reverts to its original state.

```csharp
[Test]
        public void ArrayList_Contains_OriginalState()
        {
            // Arrange
            ArrayList testList = new ArrayList();
            testList.Add(1);
            testList.Add(2);
            testList.Add(3);
            // Act
            ArrayList originalState = new ArrayList(testList);
            // Assert
            Assert.IsTrue(originalState.Count == testList.Count);

            foreach (var item in originalState)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(testList.Contains((int)item) == originalState.Contains((int)item));
            }
        }
```

## Fight Climbing Simulation

To make a fight climbing simulation, I will develop a game where two characters can engage in a match based on predefined rules. The character selection and rules will be determined by the user.

To achieve this, I will create a program that can simulate the fight between two characters based on their attributes and combat abilities. The program will also contain a set of predefined rules and guidelines for a match between these characters to decide the winner.

To start with, the program can randomly assign characters to each player. Later on, the program can randomly generate attributes for each character, and based on these attributes the program can decide on the fight characteristics for each character. The program can be expanded to include more characters, abilities, and rules in the future.

The following steps can be involved in developing this program:
1. Create a character class that holds a set of predefined attributes such as strength, speed, etc. and functions to update these attributes.
2. Create a fight class that takes two character objects as input and simulates a fight.
3. Develop the rules of the combat within the fight class. For example, the damage taken by a character can be calculated based on their strength or speed.
4. Decide on a winner between the two characters based on some rules.
5. Create a main function to manage the game - choose characters, start the fight, and display the results.

This program provides a basic starting point for a fight climbing simulation and could be extended to cover more complex functionality and game mechanics.

It's worth mentioning that a real-world fight climbing simulation would be much more sophisticated, incorporating multiple stages of play, varying terrain, weather and obstacle courses, and more complex rules based on real-life conditions. But for the purpose of this task, a simple simulated console-based game can serve as a good start.

---

